          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The flower directly presents her love for the little prince as a current emotional state. The surrounding statement that he had not known it makes this a belated avowal or disclosure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression avows love but does not itself promise continued devotion, reunion, care, or any other future action. No undertaking by the flower is encoded in the target, and no acceptance of an undertaking occurs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Although the farewell is emotionally charged, the love expression is represented as a calm, intelligible disclosure rather than a reflex-like affective outburst. Emotional circumstances and impending tears do not by themselves establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The target's core force is adequately captured as a direct avowal of love. Its belated, corrective character is part of how that avowal is presented, rather than a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her prior conduct, the flower calmly tells him that she loves him and acknowledges that her own fault prevented him from knowing it. The utterance functions as a farewell disclosure in a scene where she conceals her tears.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target contains no specific undertaking by the flower. Her surrounding words release him to pursue happiness and accept his departure rather than propose a shared commitment. The little prince gives no verbal acceptance or response to the love avowal in the supplied scene, so uptake beyond hearing it is not established.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied chapter headings, characters, and wording identify Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented presence and avowal of affection, but it does not establish any future relational terms or the little prince's internal response to this specific avowal.\"}"
        }
